WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Good morning, Hank. It’s Tuesday. Signing update: I am just past 135,000 signatures.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.880
Okay, so, ten years ago today was one of the
most important days of my life.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.880
Hank, a long time ago, you introduced me to the idea that bad news usually happens all at once

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.460
whereas good news usually happens
slowly.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.090
Like, part of the reason we don’t hear about
the continuing decline of absolute poverty

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.700
or childhood mortality or deaths by violence
is that they aren’t events, right?

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.460
They're processes that occur over decades or centuries.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.880
But from earthquakes to shootings to car 
accidents, bad news is usually an event.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.740
Like, we all know the experience of going
about our daily lives, and then, boom, out of nowhere—life-changingly bad news.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.420
However, that noted, sometimes bad news happens
slowly (climate change comes to mind)

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.480
and good news happens all at once.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.500
Like, boom, out-of-nowhere, incredibly happy
news!

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.880
Maybe you get a surprise A on a test or an
unexpected gift from a friend, or maybe it’s something bigger.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.740
And when I think about this in my own life,
there are five memories of shocked joy that come to mind.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:05.740
The first two are the births of my children,
which I suppose is not, like, technically surprising  because I did know it was coming,

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:13.480
but both times the feeling was a complete surprise: this weird overwhelmedness with a mix of joy and I guess, like, awe.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.740
The third was in 2003. Sarah and I had been friends for a couple years at that point,

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.680
and we were hanging out one afternoon at her apartment,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.260
and she mentioned that she and her boyfriend had broken up a couple months earlier, and I was like, “Wait, what?”

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.160
Fourth, it’s January of 2006, Sarah and
I living in New York, we’re engaged,

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.340
and we’re walking down the street on our way
to go register for wedding presents.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.900
And my parents happen to be in town, so they’re
with us, and my dad happened to have a camera.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.640
And you have to remember, this is before everyone
has like a camera inside their phone.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.020
Right, so I get a phone call from a weird
number, and I answer it,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:51.020
and I learn that my first novel, Looking for Alaska, has just won the Michael L. Printz Award, the biggest award in young adult books.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.220
And my dad takes this picture of us as we’re
finding out.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.120
And lastly, it’s July of 2007, we’ve just
moved from New York to Indianapolis for Sarah’s job,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.870
and we’ve just bought our first house,
which is really exciting but also really scary

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:08.280
because suddenly I have a mortgage and a lawn to maintain and gutters to clean

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.300
and there are boxes everywhere, and also I literally had no friends.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:20.240
Work is going okay; my second book didn’t sell very well, but I am doing this fun, year-long project with my brother called Brotherhood 2.0

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.240
where we make videos back and forth to each other every weekday.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.900
A few hundred people watch every day, and it’s a really creatively fulfilling project,

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.160
but it hasn’t even occurred to me to keep
going after the end of 2007.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:39.020
And then, ten years ago today, Hank, you upload a song about how excited you are for the publication of the last Harry Potter book.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:44.380
It’s called Accio—acsio? ahccio? ashkoshbegoshio?—Deathly Hallows, and it starts like this

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.340
I’m getting kind of tired of this prepublication media blitz.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.280
You got all of Mugglekind under your spell.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.240
Don’t you know the whole world’s already
gone and reserved a copy at Amazon?

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.120
How many more books could you sell?

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.840
When I first see that video, I just think,
“Wow,” and I am not alone.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.930
Lots of people like the song.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:07.880
It gets picked up on Harry Potter fan forums,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.520
which leads to our project being discovered by a group of passionate people who know how to build internet communities.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.460
And a couple days later, the song is featured on the front page of YouTube,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.520
and we go from around 200 YouTube subscribers to over 5,000.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.160
And suddenly, our year-long project starts
to feel like maybe it could last longer than a year.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.040
And here we are a decade later, so lucky to still be talking to each other and to still have people watching.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.820
Hank, without “Accio Deathly Hallows,”
it’s hard to know what our lives would look like, but they would look different.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.080
We wouldn’t have met Esther or the people
who powered the Project for Awesome.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.540
You probably wouldn’t have gotten to sing
in front of thousands of people,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.220
and I highly doubt that I would be in a position to sign
200,000 copies of my new book.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:50.690
So, Hank, thank you for making that song and
for giving me one of my favorite memories.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.860
And thanks to everyone who watched it then, and also everyone who watches us now.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.780
Hank, I’ll see you on Friday.

